# 3. Non-Blocking I/O

## What is I/O?

I/O = Input / Output Operations

### Examples

- Reading Files
- Writing Files
- Database Queries
- API Calls
- Network Requests

---

## Blocking I/O

Execution waits until the operation completes.

### Example

```js
const fs = require("fs");

const data = fs.readFileSync("test.txt", "utf-8");

console.log(data);
console.log("Finished");
```

### Output

```text
File Content
Finished
```

Execution waits until file reading finishes.

---

## Non-Blocking I/O

Node.js delegates work to Libuv.

### Example

```js
const fs = require("fs");

fs.readFile("test.txt", "utf-8", (err, data) => {
  console.log(data);
});

console.log("Finished");
```

### Output

```text
Finished
File Content
```

---

## Blocking vs Non-Blocking

| Blocking | Non-Blocking |
|-----------|-------------|
| Waits for completion | Doesn't wait |
| Synchronous | Asynchronous |
| Lower scalability | Higher scalability |
| Thread remains busy | Thread remains free |

